%S A398266 0,0,0,0,0,1,0,1,1,2,3,10,16,52
%N A398266 Number of reduced minimal planar doodle diagrams with n crossings.
%C A398266 A planar doodle is a collection of immersed circles in a plane with no triple intersection points.
%C A398266 For any irreducible doodle there is a unique diagram that has the smallest possible number of intersections — without 1- and 2-sided regions. This diagram is called minimal.
%C A398266 A component of a minimal diagram is said to be removable if its absence leads to another minimal diagram. A component that is a small circle surrounding a crossing is called a vertex circle. A minimal diagram containing no removable vertex circles is called reduced.
%H A398266 Andrew Bartholomew and Roger Fenn, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.08834">Planar Doodles: Their Properties, Codes and Classification</a>, arXiv:2308.08834 [math.GT], 2023.
